         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### In Mesoamerica\n\nNow, let's look at a structure known to its builders as *Witz* — a sacred mountain.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"img4\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img4', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 6</b>: Sacred mountain at Xunantunich, Belize. Photograph taken in <htdate0></span>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "22"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "In this sacred mountain, we see a staircase on the front face, leading to a pyramidal structure with three levels. At the top of this structure, there are windows of a room. Finally, at the highest part, there is an open area for celebrations.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "a863271d-bd7d-413b-8f07-33872be983ea",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1
      },
      {
         "id": "a863271d-bd7d-413b-8f07-33872be983ea",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "Does the Witz at Xunantunich, Belize, have exactly the shape of a pyramid?",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "36b9dbda-e84c-49cf-9b69-88e0afc97a9a",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "no",
         "score": 2
      },
      {
         "id": "36b9dbda-e84c-49cf-9b69-88e0afc97a9a",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "shocking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### It looks like one, but it isn't\n\nWe often simplify definitions to compare them with something we already know, making it something it is not.\n\nIf we simplify the concept of pyramids too much, leaving the sides as triangles that meet at the top, we might be able to call the Witz at Xunantunich a pyramid.\n\nBut, since we cannot extract these characteristics from it, the best approach would be to keep it as a sacred mountain.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "29039ebb-c1e6-42ba-b165-8094df28addb",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1
      },
      {
         "id": "29039ebb-c1e6-42ba-b165-8094df28addb",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "https://www.ceapedi.com.ar/Imagenes/Biblioteca/libreria/298.pdf",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "ffffd208-eb09-4da3-b516-f8bc695758dd",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=ffffd208-eb09-4da3-b516-f8bc695758dd",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### On precision\n\nWhen we work with precise definitions, besides improving our understanding of things, we can avoid errors like claiming that the pyramids built in Egypt are the same as those built in the Americas (*Abya Yala*).\n\nBut this is not a definitive barrier. If we talked about structures that resemble pyramids, it might be possible to group them together, but even then, we could not speak of the same origins.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "0a1473d5-2f57-4352-a7e8-7ec8cd80561e",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1
      },
      {
         "id": "0a1473d5-2f57-4352-a7e8-7ec8cd80561e",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson we learned that not everything that looks alike is the same, and even when we make simplifications to try to group things together, we still would not change their origins.\n\nFor this reason, when making comparisons, we must be careful not to make mistakes.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
